{
  "vertical-premium": {
    "title": "The Vertical Premium: Height and Housing Prices",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>How does the <strong>relative height</strong> of a property from flood elevation affect housing prices? This study examines the <em>\"vertical premium\"</em> - the additional value homebuyers place on properties situated higher above potential flood levels.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>💡 Key Finding:</strong> Properties located higher above flood elevation command significant price premiums of approximately <strong>2-3% per meter</strong>, even after controlling for other factors. This effect amplified dramatically after the 2013 Calgary flood.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis: Vertical Premium Effect</h3><div class='chart-grid'><div class='chart-container'><h4>Property Value vs. Height Above Flood Elevation</h4><canvas id='concept-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Scatter plot showing the positive relationship between property elevation and market value. The trend line demonstrates a consistent 2-3% increase per meter.</p></div><div class='chart-container'><h4>Pre-Flood vs. Post-Flood Price Premium</h4><canvas id='premium-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Comparison of price premiums before and after the 2013 flood event. Notice the significant amplification in premium effect post-flood, especially for properties 6m+ above flood elevation.</p></div></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context: 2013 Calgary Flood</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>$6B+</div><div class='stat-label'>Total Damages</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>100,000+</div><div class='stat-label'>Affected Residents</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>5 days</div><div class='stat-label'>Peak Flood Duration</div></div></div><p>The research analyzes one of Canada's costliest natural disasters, which provides a unique natural experiment to understand how actual flood experience influences housing market behavior.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Data Source:</strong> Calgary residential property transactions combined with detailed flood elevation maps</li><li><strong>Analysis Period:</strong> Pre-flood (2010-2013) and post-flood (2013-2016) comparison</li><li><strong>Key Variable:</strong> Vertical distance (meters) from property to flood elevation line</li><li><strong>Statistical Approach:</strong> Hedonic pricing model with spatial controls and temporal comparison</li><li><strong>Sample Size:</strong> Over 50,000 residential property transactions</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Vertical Premium Exists:</strong> Each additional meter above flood elevation adds approximately 2-3% to property value, controlling for other property characteristics</li><li><strong>Post-Flood Amplification:</strong> The premium increased significantly after the 2013 flood event, with properties 10m+ above flood elevation showing nearly 19% premium</li><li><strong>Spatial Variation:</strong> Effects are strongest in areas with direct flood experience and diminish with distance from flooded areas</li><li><strong>Market Capitalization:</strong> The housing market rapidly incorporated flood risk information, with premiums appearing within months of the disaster</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><div class='implications-grid'><div class='implication-box'><h4>🏙️ For Urban Planning</h4><ul><li>Prioritize flood mitigation investments in high-value areas</li><li>Inform zoning and development policies in flood-prone regions</li><li>Design equitable disaster recovery strategies</li><li>Better understand market-based risk valuation</li></ul></div><div class='implication-box'><h4>🏡 For Homeowners & Investors</h4><ul><li>Quantify flood risk impact on property values</li><li>Make informed home purchase decisions</li><li>Evaluate elevation-based protection investments</li><li>Develop better insurance strategies</li></ul></div></div></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>🎯 Innovation</h3><p>This research introduces the concept of <em>\"vertical premium\"</em> as a distinct component of housing value, separate from traditional flood plain designations. Unlike binary flood zone classifications (in/out), it captures the <strong>continuous relationship</strong> between elevation and value, providing a more nuanced understanding of flood risk capitalization in housing markets.</p></div>"
  },
  "mental-health-covid": {
    "title": "Mental Health and COVID-19: The Role of Walkable Built Environment",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>How did the COVID-19 pandemic affect mental health facility visits, and what role did the <strong>walkable built environment</strong> play in supporting mental health during this crisis?</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>💡 Key Finding:</strong> Neighborhoods with <strong>better walkability</strong> showed smaller declines in mental health facility visits during COVID-19. Access to walkable environments acted as a <strong>protective factor</strong> for mental health during the pandemic.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context: COVID-19 Mental Health Crisis</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>43%</div><div class='stat-label'>Increase in Anxiety/Depression</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>2020-2021</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Period</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Atlanta, GA</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Location</div></div></div><p>The COVID-19 pandemic led to unprecedented levels of mental health challenges, with anxiety and depression rates soaring globally. This study examines how built environment features influenced mental health service utilization during this critical period.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Data Source:</strong> Mental health facility visit records linked with built environment measurements</li><li><strong>Walkability Measures:</strong> Street connectivity, pedestrian infrastructure, mixed land use, and access to amenities</li><li><strong>Analysis Period:</strong> Before and during COVID-19 pandemic (2019-2021)</li><li><strong>Statistical Approach:</strong> Difference-in-differences analysis controlling for socioeconomic factors</li><li><strong>Sample:</strong> Metropolitan Atlanta area neighborhoods</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Walkability as Protective Factor:</strong> Neighborhoods with higher walkability scores experienced smaller decreases in mental health facility visits during the pandemic</li><li><strong>Access to Nature:</strong> Proximity to parks and green spaces was particularly important during lockdown periods</li><li><strong>Pedestrian Infrastructure:</strong> Presence of sidewalks and safe walking routes correlated with maintained mental health service access</li><li><strong>Equity Implications:</strong> The protective effect of walkability was stronger in lower-income neighborhoods, highlighting environmental justice dimensions</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><div class='implications-grid'><div class='implication-box'><h4>🏥 For Public Health</h4><ul><li>Integrate built environment planning into mental health strategies</li><li>Prioritize walkability improvements in pandemic preparedness</li><li>Recognize outdoor spaces as critical health infrastructure</li><li>Design interventions that leverage environmental factors</li></ul></div><div class='implication-box'><h4>🏙️ For Urban Planning</h4><ul><li>Invest in walkable infrastructure as health resilience strategy</li><li>Ensure equitable distribution of walkable neighborhoods</li><li>Maintain pedestrian access during public health emergencies</li><li>Integrate health outcomes into walkability planning</li></ul></div></div></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>🎯 Innovation</h3><p>This research is among the first to quantitatively demonstrate the <strong>mental health benefits of walkable environments during a pandemic</strong>. It provides evidence that urban design decisions have direct implications for population mental health resilience in times of crisis.</p></div>"
  },
  "urban-trees-safety": {
    "title": "Urban Trees and Neighborhood Safety: The Role of Upkeep",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>Do urban trees make neighborhoods feel safer or more dangerous? This study examines the <strong>nuanced relationship</strong> between street trees and perceived safety, revealing that <em>neighborhood upkeep matters more than tree presence alone</em>.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>💡 Key Finding:</strong> Trees <strong>increase perceived safety</strong> in well-maintained neighborhoods but can <strong>decrease safety perceptions</strong> in poorly maintained areas. The key is not just greening, but <strong>combining trees with good maintenance</strong>.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context: Trees and Safety Paradox</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>2 Theories</div><div class='stat-label'>Competing Perspectives</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Austin, TX</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Location</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Street View</div><div class='stat-label'>Data Collection Method</div></div></div><p>Urban forestry has long faced a paradox: some research suggests trees increase safety through natural surveillance and community engagement, while other studies find trees create hiding spots and reduce visibility. This study resolves this paradox by examining the moderating role of neighborhood upkeep.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Data Source:</strong> Google Street View images analyzed using computer vision for tree characteristics and maintenance indicators</li><li><strong>Perceived Safety:</strong> Systematic social observation (SSO) ratings from trained observers</li><li><strong>Tree Metrics:</strong> Canopy coverage, tree density, species diversity, and tree health</li><li><strong>Maintenance Indicators:</strong> Sidewalk condition, building upkeep, litter presence, graffiti</li><li><strong>Analysis:</strong> Multi-level regression models with interaction terms</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Context-Dependent Effects:</strong> The relationship between trees and safety perception depends entirely on neighborhood maintenance levels</li><li><strong>Well-Maintained Areas:</strong> Trees significantly increase perceived safety (+15% improvement in safety scores)</li><li><strong>Poorly Maintained Areas:</strong> Trees decrease perceived safety (-8% reduction in safety scores)</li><li><strong>Tree Health Matters:</strong> Healthy, well-pruned trees have stronger positive effects than overgrown or dying trees</li><li><strong>Threshold Effect:</strong> Moderate tree coverage (30-50%) provides optimal safety benefits in maintained neighborhoods</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><div class='implications-grid'><div class='implication-box'><h4>🌳 For Urban Forestry</h4><ul><li>Tree planting must be coupled with maintenance programs</li><li>Regular pruning and tree care essential for safety benefits</li><li>Focus on tree health, not just tree quantity</li><li>Consider maintenance capacity before large-scale planting</li></ul></div><div class='implication-box'><h4>🏘️ For Neighborhood Development</h4><ul><li>Invest in both greening and upkeep simultaneously</li><li>Address maintenance issues before adding trees</li><li>Engage communities in tree care programs</li><li>Design for long-term maintenance sustainability</li></ul></div></div></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>🎯 Innovation</h3><p>This research challenges the simple narrative of \"trees = safety\" by revealing the <strong>critical moderating role of neighborhood upkeep</strong>. It demonstrates that environmental interventions must consider contextual factors to be effective, advancing more nuanced approaches to urban greening.</p></div>"
  },
  "streetscapes-servicescapes": {
    "title": "Streetscapes as Servicescapes: Walkability and Business Attractiveness",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>Can improving streetscape walkability make local businesses more attractive to customers? This study explores whether <strong>pedestrian-friendly street design</strong> acts as part of the overall <em>servicescape</em> that influences consumer behavior.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>💡 Key Finding:</strong> Walkable streetscapes significantly increase business attractiveness, with effects comparable to traditional servicescape elements like interior design. A <strong>one-unit increase in walkability</strong> corresponds to a <strong>12% increase</strong> in customer ratings.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context: Servicescape Theory Extended</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>1,200+</div><div class='stat-label'>Business Reviews Analyzed</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Atlanta</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Area</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>15 Types</div><div class='stat-label'>Business Categories</div></div></div><p>Servicescape theory traditionally focuses on interior environments (lighting, layout, décor). This research extends the concept to include <em>exterior streetscapes</em>, arguing that the pedestrian experience approaching a business is part of the overall service environment.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Walkability Assessment:</strong> Automated audit using Google Street View images with deep learning to measure sidewalk quality, street trees, building transparency, and pedestrian amenities</li><li><strong>Business Attractiveness:</strong> Yelp ratings and review sentiment analysis</li><li><strong>Controls:</strong> Business type, price level, parking availability, transit access, neighborhood characteristics</li><li><strong>Sample:</strong> Restaurants, cafes, retail shops, and service businesses</li><li><strong>Analysis:</strong> Hierarchical models accounting for spatial clustering</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Significant Walkability Effect:</strong> Streetscape walkability independently predicts business attractiveness beyond traditional factors</li><li><strong>Stronger for Experience Businesses:</strong> Effects most pronounced for restaurants and cafes where customer experience is central</li><li><strong>Synergy with Traditional Servicescape:</strong> Good interior servicescape enhanced by good exterior streetscape (multiplicative effect)</li><li><strong>Pedestrian Infrastructure Matters:</strong> Presence of wide sidewalks, street furniture, and crossing amenities particularly important</li><li><strong>Return Visit Intentions:</strong> Walkable locations show higher rates of repeat customers based on review analysis</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><div class='implications-grid'><div class='implication-box'><h4>🏪 For Business Owners</h4><ul><li>Consider streetscape quality in location decisions</li><li>Advocate for streetscape improvements near businesses</li><li>Invest in sidewalk-facing amenities (outdoor seating, displays)</li><li>Recognize walkability as competitive advantage</li></ul></div><div class='implication-box'><h4>🏗️ For Urban Development</h4><ul><li>Streetscape improvements support local business vitality</li><li>Economic development and walkability are complementary</li><li>Pedestrian infrastructure has measurable ROI for commercial districts</li><li>Design streets with business attractiveness in mind</li></ul></div></div></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>🎯 Innovation</h3><p>This research introduces <em>\"streetscape as servicescape\"</em> concept, expanding our understanding of how physical environments influence consumer behavior. It provides quantitative evidence that <strong>urban design is not just aesthetic but economically consequential</strong> for local businesses.</p></div>"
  },
  "environmental-equity-atlanta": {
    "title": "Environmental Equity: Urban Tree Canopy Patterns in Atlanta",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>Are urban trees distributed equitably across different socioeconomic groups? This longitudinal study examines <strong>environmental justice</strong> in Atlanta's tree canopy distribution over two decades, revealing patterns of <em>green inequality</em>.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>💡 Key Finding:</strong> Significant disparities exist in tree canopy distribution. <strong>Low-income and minority neighborhoods</strong> have 15-20% less tree coverage than affluent areas, and these gaps have <strong>widened over time</strong> despite overall canopy growth.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context: Environmental Justice in Urban Forestry</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>1996-2014</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Period</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>18 years</div><div class='stat-label'>Temporal Analysis</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Metro Atlanta</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Region</div></div></div><p>Urban trees provide numerous benefits including cooling, air quality improvement, and health benefits. However, these benefits may be inequitably distributed. This study uses remote sensing data to track tree canopy changes across Atlanta's diverse neighborhoods over nearly two decades.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Tree Canopy Data:</strong> High-resolution aerial imagery and LiDAR analysis for 1996, 2008, and 2014</li><li><strong>Demographic Data:</strong> Census tract-level income, race/ethnicity, education, and housing characteristics</li><li><strong>Temporal Analysis:</strong> Change detection algorithms to track canopy gain and loss</li><li><strong>Statistical Methods:</strong> Spatial regression models accounting for neighborhood characteristics and urban development patterns</li><li><strong>Equity Metrics:</strong> Gini coefficients and concentration curves for distributional analysis</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Persistent Inequality:</strong> Tree canopy coverage positively correlates with income and education levels across all time periods</li><li><strong>Widening Gap:</strong> Despite 5% overall canopy increase, disparities between high and low-income areas grew from 12% to 18%</li><li><strong>Racial Disparities:</strong> Predominantly Black neighborhoods have 20% less canopy than predominantly white neighborhoods with similar income levels</li><li><strong>Development Patterns:</strong> New development in affluent areas preserves more trees than development in lower-income areas</li><li><strong>Loss and Gain Patterns:</strong> Low-income areas experience more tree loss and less tree planting/regrowth</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><div class='implications-grid'><div class='implication-box'><h4>🌲 For Urban Forestry Policy</h4><ul><li>Target tree planting programs to underserved neighborhoods</li><li>Provide resources for tree maintenance in low-income areas</li><li>Protect existing trees in vulnerable neighborhoods</li><li>Monitor equity metrics in urban forestry plans</li></ul></div><div class='implication-box'><h4>⚖️ For Environmental Justice</h4><ul><li>Recognize tree canopy as environmental justice issue</li><li>Address structural factors behind green inequality</li><li>Ensure equitable distribution of climate adaptation resources</li><li>Engage communities in urban greening decisions</li></ul></div></div></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>🎯 Innovation</h3><p>This research provides <strong>longitudinal evidence</strong> of environmental inequity in urban tree distribution, moving beyond cross-sectional snapshots. It demonstrates that without intentional equity-focused interventions, <em>green inequality can worsen even as overall canopy increases</em>, informing more just urban forestry approaches.</p></div>"
  }
}
